======================================================================================================================
8. can the 3-D position dissolve the EFE?  the first-infall escape, computed
======================================================================================================================
  Nagesh+2024 (A&A 690, A149), simulating exactly these objects in MOND with Phantom of Ramses, found
  that cluster tides CANNOT raise the dispersions to the observed values for UDGs in equilibrium, and
  that the surviving MOND option is a first radial infall -- the dispersion then reflects the far weaker
  external field near turnaround, not the field at the instantaneous position.  Priced here.
    assumed 3-D radius   g_ext/a0    y_extN  nu(1+L/3)|ext   EFE offset
                0.9 Mpc      1.059    0.5570          1.688       +1.258
                1.5 Mpc      0.653    0.2612          2.180       +1.148
                2.3 Mpc      0.427    0.1289          2.856       +1.035
                4.0 Mpc      0.252    0.0509          4.217       +0.876
                6.0 Mpc      0.168    0.0243          5.861       +0.751
                9.0 Mpc      0.112    0.0114          8.328       +0.635
               15.0 Mpc      0.067    0.0043         13.266       +0.520
  [PASS] E5 the external field CANNOT be made to go away for a bound Coma member.  Even at 9 Mpc -- about four virial radii, i.e. the turnaround scale, which is the most generous first-infall geometry -- the EFE still costs the framework a real offset, and the isolated limit is not reached at any radius a Coma member can occupy   (EFE offset at 9 Mpc = +0.635 dex vs the isolated floor +0.396 dex; the equilibrium arm is +1.159 dex)

